hi there i'm going to show you some of  my favorite japanese recipes to make  during summer time  june is japan's rainy season it's not a  pleasant time  but the rain brings out japan's natural  beauty  especially in plants like trees and  flowers  one particularly loved flower in japan  is the hyderanja  and they are beautiful during this  season  a hydrangeas color depends on the ph  level of soil  so if the soil is acidic pet outstand  blue  if the soil is neutral or basic they  tend to have pink or  red color we used red cabbage as natural  food dye  red cabbage also acts similarly to  hydrangeas  when you simmer with water the water  turns purple  but if you add lemon it turns pink  i made a color changing cocktail video  before using the same trick  some people were worried that it would  taste like cabbage  but we are using such a small amount  that it won't affect the flavor  the tasty japan team made a traditional  japanese confectionary  or as we call them wagashi wagashi  designs reflect the seasons  so this hydrangea design is perfect for  the early summer  wagashi is usually solved with tea  it pairs well with bitter matcha if you  join the tea ceremony  you will recognize wagashi's designs  differ from season to  season the purpose of the tea ceremony  is not only to enjoy tea and wagashi  but also enjoy the changes of season  on july 7th japan celebrates tanabata  a star festival and it's based on an  old folktale these folk tales main  characters are two lovers  orihime the princess who lived the  clouds  and hikoboshi a celestial cowherd  according to the folk tale they are  separated by the milky way  and they can only meet once in a year  which is july 7th  at this festival we write down a wish on  rectangular paper  and tie it with a string to a bamboo  tree  the folktale and celebration originated  as a chinese folk tale  they also celebrate july 7th in china  but according to my chinese co-worker  jin  they celebrate a little bit differently  nowadays  it's more like valentine's day in china  so  couples exchange gifts on july 7th  i've also heard that it's celebrated  across asia  so leave a note in the comments about  how you celebrate  in japan traditionally we so many  noodles to celebrate tanabata  but milky way-inspired or star-shaped  foods are very popular way to celebrate  this day  for this video the tasty japan team made  no-bake  cheesecake with blue agar jelly they  also used  silver sprinkles so it looks like stars  floating in the milky way  my co-worker alby made this video when  we went to japan a couple years ago  tonkatsu or fried pork is a popular  topping for curry  we eat curry all year round but for me  japanese curry reminds me of  summer in japan i think it's because we  go camping every summer  and we make curry during our camping  trip  curry is a staple camping food in japan  as well as a quick  and easy meal to cook when you don't  have a lot of time  curry's main ingredients are onion  potato carrot and meat  curry root cubes are sold at any grocery  store in japan  we normally use these blocks so don't  worry  you aren't missing out by not making it  from scratch  you can easily find it at the asian  grocery store in the states  the taste is sweeter and not too spicy  than  indian curry it's a different experience  so definitely try it for an easy weekday  meal  here chichuka is chilled noodles usually  topped with a thinly sliced egg omelet  cucumber ham and tomato  it's a summer staple noodle dish and can  only be found  during the summer you know that sama is  around the corner  when ramen shops begin to hang banners  and posters  to let customers know that here strugga  is finally available  when the weather is too hot you'll tend  to lose your appetite  but here's chuka is a perfect dish for  that  it's light and cold and this sweet tangy  sauce will stimulate your appetite  the most popular style is soy sauce and  vinegar based sauce  but there is another popular style with  a sesame based  sauce it's creamier than the soy  sauce-based  sauce but both are very popular samadhi  sheets  sama in japan is very humid but there  are lots of food that you can only find  during summer like this hiyashiyuka  it gets really hot during summer time in  japan and here in l.a  but wherever you are i hope you will try  these recipes  and have a delicious and refreshing  summer  oh yes  [Music]  you
 

